WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.316
To reason about RAM-backed booting, it helps to understand the normal Linux boot path. At a practical level, firmware starts the machine.

2
00:00:09.316 --> 00:00:14.938
On modern systems this is often UEFI, though older systems may use BIOS-style boot.

3
00:00:14.938 --> 00:00:21.202
Firmware initializes enough hardware to find a boot target and hand control to a bootloader.

4
00:00:21.202 --> 00:00:34.212
The bootloader, such as GRUB, systemd-boot, or another distribution-specific loader, presents entries and loads the Linux kernel, usually with a kernel command line and an initramfs image.

5
00:00:34.212 --> 00:00:42.484
The kernel starts next. It initializes hardware, memory management, device drivers, and the early environment it needs.

6
00:00:42.484 --> 00:00:45.616
The initramfs is then used as early user space.

7
00:00:45.616 --> 00:00:56.378
It can load modules, unlock encrypted devices, assemble storage, discover root devices, prepare live-media layers, or run distribution-specific scripts.

8
00:00:56.378 --> 00:01:06.256
Eventually the system finds, mounts, or constructs the real root filesystem and switches from early user space into the normal operating system.

9
00:01:06.256 --> 00:01:09.629
RAM-boot designs usually affect this early path.

10
00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:17.178
A live system that supports a toram option may use initramfs logic to copy the compressed live image into memory.

11
00:01:17.178 --> 00:01:26.976
An overlay design may have early boot scripts that mount a read-only lower layer and a writable tmpfs upper layer before presenting a merged root.

12
00:01:26.976 --> 00:01:36.774
A volatile root design may change what is mounted as persistent storage and what is recreated on each boot. This is why exact commands vary so much.

13
00:01:36.774 --> 00:01:49.704
The safe method depends on the distribution, bootloader, initramfs framework, filesystem layout, encrypted disk setup, secure boot configuration, hardware drivers, and storage naming.

14
00:01:49.704 --> 00:01:55.325
A kernel parameter that works on one live distribution may mean nothing on another.

15
00:01:55.325 --> 00:02:04.561
A bootloader edit that is harmless on a virtual machine can make a laptop hard to recover if there is no known-good entry or recovery media.

16
00:02:04.561 --> 00:02:12.432
The lesson is conceptual first. Know where the bootloader, kernel, initramfs, root filesystem, and user space fit.

17
00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:19.178
Then test changes in a virtual machine, spare disk, or lab system before touching a primary machine.
